To solve this problem, you need to implement a **Global Tooltip Provider**.

Since Next.js components (especially in Server Components or different pages) are isolated, you cannot rely on local state. Instead, you will create a Context that holds the state of the *currently active* tooltip. When a user opens a tooltip, you update the context; when they close it, you update the context again.

Here is a complete, copy-pasteable solution using **React Context** and **Tailwind CSS** (standard in Next.js).

### 1. Create the Context and Provider

First, create a file to manage the global state. This will track which tooltip is currently open.

`app/providers/tooltip-provider.tsx` (or `components/tooltip-provider.tsx`)

```tsx
"use client";

import React, { createContext, useContext, useState, ReactNode } from "react";

// Define the shape of our context
interface TooltipContextType {
  activeTooltipId: string | null;
  openTooltip: (id: string) => void;
  closeTooltip: () => void;
}

const TooltipContext = createContext<TooltipContextType | undefined>(undefined);

export const TooltipProvider = ({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) => {
  const [activeTooltipId, setActiveTooltipId] = useState<string | null>(null);

  const openTooltip = (id: string) => {
    // If a tooltip is already open, close it first (optional, but good for UX)
    // setActiveTooltipId(id); 
    setActiveTooltipId(id);
  };

  const closeTooltip = () => {
    setActiveTooltipId(null);
  };

  return (
    <TooltipContext.Provider value={{ activeTooltipId, openTooltip, closeTooltip }}>
      {children}
    </TooltipContext.Provider>
  );
};

export const useTooltip = () => {
  const context = useContext(TooltipContext);
  if (!context) {
    throw new Error("useTooltip must be used within a TooltipProvider");
  }
  return context;
};
```

### 2. Create the Tooltip Component

This component uses the context to check if it should render. If the `activeTooltipId` matches the component's ID, it renders.

`app/components/tooltip.tsx`

```tsx
"use client";

import React, { useEffect, useRef } from "react";
import { useTooltip } from "@/app/providers/tooltip-provider"; // Adjust path as needed

interface TooltipProps {
  id: string;
  content: string;
  children: React.ReactNode;
  position?: "top" | "bottom" | "left" | "right";
}

export const Tooltip = ({ id, content, children, position = "top" }: TooltipProps) => {
  const { activeTooltipId, openTooltip, closeTooltip } = useTooltip();
  const isHovered = activeTooltipId === id;
  const tooltipRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  // Handle clicking outside to close
  useEffect(() => {
    const handleClickOutside = (event: MouseEvent) => {
      if (tooltipRef.current && !tooltipRef.current.contains(event.target as Node)) {
        closeTooltip();
      }
    };

    document.addEventListener("mousedown", handleClickOutside);
    return () => document.removeEventListener("mousedown", handleClickOutside);
  }, [closeTooltip]);

  const handleMouseEnter = () => {
    openTooltip(id);
  };

  const handleMouseLeave = () => {
    closeTooltip();
