Here are the three most effective methods, ranging from simple to robust.

### Method 1: The `Map` Approach (Best for Simple Games)
This is the most straightforward method. You use a `HashMap` where the **Key** is the current dialogue ID, and the **Value** is a list of `DialogueOption` objects.

This approach is great for static conversations where the flow is linear or has simple branches.

#### 1. Define the Data Structure
Create a class to hold the options.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class DialogueOption {
    private String text;
    private String nextDialogueId; // The ID of the dialogue to show next

    public DialogueOption(String text, String nextDialogueId) {
        this.text = text;
        this.nextDialogueId = nextDialogueId;
    }

    public String getText() { return text; }
    public String getNextDialogueId() { return nextDialogueId; }
}
```

#### 2. Create the Dialogue Manager
This class holds the map of all conversations.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

class DialogueManager {
    private Map<String, List<DialogueOption>> dialogues;

    public DialogueManager() {
        this.dialogues = new HashMap<>();
    }

    // Helper to add a conversation
    public void addDialogue(String id, List<DialogueOption> options) {
        this.dialogues.put(id, options);
    }

    // Get the options for a specific ID
    public List<DialogueOption> getOptions(String id) {
        return dialogues.get(id);
    }
}
